00:07:02.390 --> 00:07:05.140
And they are the
migration to Chromebook--

00:07:05.140 --> 00:07:07.070
I will speak in
detail about this

00:07:07.070 --> 00:07:08.860
in one of the three programs.

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:11.600
Develop, as I say,
modern application web

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:14.990
apps by using Google
Cloud Services.

00:07:14.990 --> 00:07:18.700
Be serverless and
data centerless.

00:07:18.700 --> 00:07:25.060
It's a very huge world, and
it will have an impact for IT,

00:07:25.060 --> 00:07:27.790
so you will understand why.

00:07:27.790 --> 00:07:30.910
I just want to talk to you about
the cloud strategy we have.

00:07:30.910 --> 00:07:35.240
And I have made the choice to
choose only five main points,

00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:37.810
and I hope my
boss-- who is here--

00:07:37.810 --> 00:07:40.060
will agree that they are
not the only one points.

00:07:40.060 --> 00:07:45.520
But this is a very
quick presentation,

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:50.200
so I don't need to
speak to you more.

00:07:50.200 --> 00:07:52.840
So I have chosen to speak to
you about these five points

00:07:52.840 --> 00:07:54.640
because I think they
are very interesting.

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:56.140
First is no-ops.

00:07:56.140 --> 00:07:58.060
And what we say with no-ops?

00:07:58.060 --> 00:08:01.420
We say we want to let our
developers-- like me--

00:08:01.420 --> 00:08:05.830
to focus on the cloud and not
on managing the infrastructure.

00:08:05.830 --> 00:08:09.430
We think Google is
doing this very good.

00:08:09.430 --> 00:08:12.610
So why we need to spend
some time on this?

00:08:12.610 --> 00:08:15.910
We choose to use the
maximum micro-services

00:08:15.910 --> 00:08:20.950
we can use to re-use again
and again these services.

00:08:20.950 --> 00:08:22.900
We use Cloud Function,
we use App Engine,

00:08:22.900 --> 00:08:24.300
we use a lot of services.

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:26.650
I will show you some
architectural diagram

00:08:26.650 --> 00:08:27.280
after this.

00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:29.510
You will see that is true.

00:08:29.510 --> 00:08:33.490
So no-ops of course
means serverless,

00:08:33.490 --> 00:08:35.950
because Cloud Function
is a serverless service.

00:08:35.950 --> 00:08:39.400
And at the end, that
means no data center.

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:42.830
From this year, we want to
have no data center in France

00:08:42.830 --> 00:08:45.620
in the name of Veolia,
and in the next two years

00:08:45.620 --> 00:08:49.680
it will be the same
all over the world.

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:52.685
So by doing this we use
Google Cloud products.

00:08:52.685 --> 00:08:55.790
This mean secure
by design products.

00:08:55.790 --> 00:08:58.370
We want to seek to have
secure by design application,

00:08:58.370 --> 00:09:01.970
and this is why we would choose
App Engine, we choose BigQuery,

00:09:01.970 --> 00:09:04.610
we choose Cloud Function,
we choose all services

00:09:04.610 --> 00:09:05.790
provided by Google.

00:09:05.790 --> 00:09:08.877
And yes-- as Ameet said--

00:09:08.877 --> 00:09:11.210
we have made the choice to
only have two different cloud

00:09:11.210 --> 00:09:14.924
providers, they are AWS and GCP.

00:09:14.924 --> 00:09:16.340
So I have the
authorization to put

00:09:16.340 --> 00:09:21.290
the logo on this presentation,
so there is no problem.

00:09:21.290 --> 00:09:26.290
Now, about the three
programs I mentioned before.

00:09:26.290 --> 00:09:28.230
We want to create a
digital enterprise.

00:09:28.230 --> 00:09:30.840
And create a digital
enterprise is not

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:34.610
as easy as speaking
to you today.

00:09:34.610 --> 00:09:38.700
These three programs have
a lot of different detail,

00:09:38.700 --> 00:09:45.070
and I have chosen to only record
these details in three boxes.

00:09:45.070 --> 00:09:46.950
The first is the Move
to Cloud program.

00:09:46.950 --> 00:09:51.090
The Move to Cloud is for the
IT entity for the branch,

00:09:51.090 --> 00:09:54.270
and we want to help them
to move to the cloud.

00:09:54.270 --> 00:09:57.750
For me, the biggest
keywords of this program

00:09:57.750 --> 00:10:00.330
is software as a service
first, because we

00:10:00.330 --> 00:10:02.221
think this is the future.

00:10:02.221 --> 00:10:04.470
And as I said, we don't want
to manage infrastructure,

00:10:04.470 --> 00:10:06.210
we don't want to manage.

00:10:06.210 --> 00:10:10.710
We just want to focus on the
application and on the customer

00:10:10.710 --> 00:10:12.690
added value we can have.

00:10:12.690 --> 00:10:15.060
The second program
is Data For Business.

00:10:15.060 --> 00:10:18.000
And Data For Business
for me means datalake,

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:21.650
because the data is the
key of your application.

00:10:21.650 --> 00:10:24.350
And the last one is SATAWAD.

00:10:24.350 --> 00:10:28.220
And for SATAWAD-- and I will
talk to this in detail after

00:10:28.220 --> 00:10:29.110
this slide--

00:10:29.110 --> 00:10:31.110
for me it's collaboration.

00:10:31.110 --> 00:10:35.240
SATAWAD is for the employee,
and we want to, as I said,

00:10:35.240 --> 00:10:38.570
bring them to a new, modern
on the world of application,

00:10:38.570 --> 00:10:41.300
and new, modern ways to work.

00:10:41.300 --> 00:10:43.550
So in detail, SATAWAD,
what does that mean?

00:10:43.550 --> 00:10:47.900
SATAWAD means Secure, Any
Time, Anywhere, Any Device.

00:10:47.900 --> 00:10:51.080
And it's very simple, because
as you may know in your company

00:10:51.080 --> 00:10:55.880
today, your employee may want
to work from any time, anywhere,

00:10:55.880 --> 00:10:57.200
and any devices.

00:10:57.200 --> 00:11:01.970
They don't want it to
be mandatory to work

00:11:01.970 --> 00:11:02.690
at the office.

00:11:02.690 --> 00:11:04.760
They want to work from home.

00:11:04.760 --> 00:11:06.690
They want to walk
from where they want.

00:11:06.690 --> 00:11:12.440
So we propose a global
migration program to Chromebook.

00:11:12.440 --> 00:11:17.750
We propose to give a secure
and collaborative workplace.

00:11:17.750 --> 00:11:21.980
This is why we have chosen to
use G Suite for the messaging

00:11:21.980 --> 00:11:24.070
system.

00:11:24.070 --> 00:11:26.120
The second program,
Move to Cloud,

00:11:26.120 --> 00:11:30.540
is for the IT entity, and for
the entity too, of course,

00:11:30.540 --> 00:11:34.170
because we want them
to go to the cloud.

00:11:34.170 --> 00:11:36.610
We are in a
decentralized company.

00:11:36.610 --> 00:11:40.770
It means before we
were on the model

00:11:40.770 --> 00:11:42.840
when we say, hey,
this is mandatory.

00:11:42.840 --> 00:11:44.070
This is the new way.

00:11:44.070 --> 00:11:46.540
You need to work like
that and not like that.

00:11:46.540 --> 00:11:48.810
But now we are not in this way.

00:11:48.810 --> 00:11:50.590
We are on a selective adoption.

00:11:50.590 --> 00:11:52.620
We are on a way
where we say, hey,

00:11:52.620 --> 00:11:57.660
you know you can make
an evolution of your IT?

00:11:57.660 --> 00:12:01.500
You have some offer like
this, some offer like that.

00:12:01.500 --> 00:12:03.010
We can help you.

00:12:03.010 --> 00:12:05.980
We can make this for you.

00:12:05.980 --> 00:12:07.450
We can give you advice.

00:12:07.450 --> 00:12:09.460
We can give you support.

00:12:09.460 --> 00:12:10.850
It's your choice.

00:12:10.850 --> 00:12:13.190
And this is not the
same way to work.

00:12:13.190 --> 00:12:17.840
And you can trust me, this is
a very more better way to work.

00:12:17.840 --> 00:12:20.590
So this operation can
be made in two steps,

00:12:20.590 --> 00:12:23.800
because sometimes you have a
landscape of application-- very

00:12:23.800 --> 00:12:26.530
difficult, because
companies like Veolia

00:12:26.530 --> 00:12:28.670
are older companies,
so they have

00:12:28.670 --> 00:12:30.910
a big legacy of application.

00:12:30.910 --> 00:12:33.440
So we propose a managed
services and an infrastructure

00:12:33.440 --> 00:12:35.020
as a service solution.

00:12:35.020 --> 00:12:37.720
But the final jump
to the cloud for us

00:12:37.720 --> 00:12:42.340
is, as I said, the solution
as a service and the platform

00:12:42.340 --> 00:12:43.330
as a service.

00:12:43.330 --> 00:12:45.500
If you want you can
develop an application.

00:12:45.500 --> 00:12:47.160
Or if you don't
want, you can buy

00:12:47.160 --> 00:12:50.330
a solution that is ready to go.

00:12:50.330 --> 00:12:51.080
How do we do that?

00:12:51.080 --> 00:12:53.630
We do that in some steps.

00:12:53.630 --> 00:12:56.570
I have chosen to [INAUDIBLE]
them in six steps.

00:12:56.570 --> 00:13:00.020
But quickly, we help
them to discover

00:13:00.020 --> 00:13:03.620
between what is the
application they have

00:13:03.620 --> 00:13:06.650
and what are the
offers they can choose.

00:13:06.650 --> 00:13:10.790
After this, we help them to
design a new architecture

00:13:10.790 --> 00:13:12.620
of the application.

00:13:12.620 --> 00:13:14.630
We propose to them
to help them to build

00:13:14.630 --> 00:13:17.220
the new version of this
application, to transform

00:13:17.220 --> 00:13:19.810
this with us or with
some Google partners,

00:13:19.810 --> 00:13:21.680
of course, if you want.

00:13:21.680 --> 00:13:23.930
And after this we
are going with them

00:13:23.930 --> 00:13:26.210
to choose who will be
in charge to operate.

00:13:26.210 --> 00:13:28.040
If this is a managed
services, that

00:13:28.040 --> 00:13:30.740
will be maybe a cloud
provider like Google,

00:13:30.740 --> 00:13:33.680
or that can be a
separate company.

00:13:33.680 --> 00:13:37.880
And after all this, sometimes
we can optimize and optimize

00:13:37.880 --> 00:13:42.440
the application for the user
experience, or for the price

00:13:42.440 --> 00:13:45.770
and the cost of
this application.

00:13:45.770 --> 00:13:48.580
So now you know these
two main programs.

00:13:48.580 --> 00:13:52.970
We will speak about the last
one, this is Data for Business.

00:13:52.970 --> 00:13:55.390
But before speaking
about the data,

00:13:55.390 --> 00:13:59.140
I wanted to show you
how we use Google Cloud.

00:13:59.140 --> 00:14:04.420
And today we are
almost ready to say

00:14:04.420 --> 00:14:09.160
that we have more than 100
applications on Google Cloud.

00:14:09.160 --> 00:14:12.310
It can be very simple
and small application

00:14:12.310 --> 00:14:14.650
to a very big and
complex application,

00:14:14.650 --> 00:14:16.270
and I will present
to you some demos

00:14:16.270 --> 00:14:18.190
of applications we have made.

00:14:18.190 --> 00:14:21.700
And this represents 500 App
Engine services, for example,

00:14:21.700 --> 00:14:25.310
and almost 5,000
BigQuery datasets.

00:14:25.310 --> 00:14:28.660
But the interesting point
on this is the security.

00:14:28.660 --> 00:14:32.050
But as you can see on my
slide, we have 1,000 projects.

00:14:32.050 --> 00:14:34.240
And 1,000 projects--
you can trust me--

00:14:34.240 --> 00:14:35.950
is very big.

00:14:35.950 --> 00:14:38.670
You need to secure,
you need to monitor,

00:14:38.670 --> 00:14:40.180
you need to check
that everything

00:14:40.180 --> 00:14:42.010
is OK on your infrastructure.

00:14:42.010 --> 00:14:44.260
By infrastructure I mean
the Google Cloud platform,

00:14:44.260 --> 00:14:45.550
of course.

00:14:45.550 --> 00:14:48.580
So we have a lot of
AAN policy to do that.

00:14:48.580 --> 00:14:50.320
We have a lot of firewalls.

00:14:50.320 --> 00:14:52.420
We are using a lot of
different services,

00:14:52.420 --> 00:14:55.000
and I will speak in detail
about these services

00:14:55.000 --> 00:14:57.200
at the end of my presentation.

00:14:57.200 --> 00:15:00.790
But now you know how
we use Google Cloud.

00:15:00.790 --> 00:15:05.170
Let's talk about the
data, because as I said,

00:15:05.170 --> 00:15:06.420
the data is the key.

00:15:06.420 --> 00:15:09.690
And we think in Veolia
that having the datalake

00:15:09.690 --> 00:15:10.617
is mandatory.

00:15:10.617 --> 00:15:12.450
And you can see some
interesting white paper

00:15:12.450 --> 00:15:15.420
on Google Cloud's
website on this,

00:15:15.420 --> 00:15:19.270
and it's very close
to our approach.

00:15:19.270 --> 00:15:22.850
And you probably asked me about
why we choose Google Cloud,

00:15:22.850 --> 00:15:24.630
and why we create a datalake.

00:15:24.630 --> 00:15:27.470
And I will show to you
that the datalake for us

00:15:27.470 --> 00:15:32.300
is having a central point
of data from whatever

00:15:32.300 --> 00:15:36.980
is there to analyze,
process, and extract

00:15:36.980 --> 00:15:38.870
an added value of this data.

00:15:38.870 --> 00:15:40.900
And why we chose Google Cloud?

00:15:40.900 --> 00:15:42.230
It's very simple.

00:15:42.230 --> 00:15:45.240
Because it's very cheap,
this is very fast,

00:15:45.240 --> 00:15:48.530
and this is very easy to use.

00:15:48.530 --> 00:15:51.500
So how is made, our datalake?

00:15:51.500 --> 00:15:54.290
In just four main steps.

00:15:54.290 --> 00:15:57.040
First, we acquire
other data, as I

00:15:57.040 --> 00:15:59.050
said, from whatever is there--

00:15:59.050 --> 00:16:01.720
from application,
other cloud provider--

00:16:01.720 --> 00:16:06.440
I didn't mention any name-- from
user data, from the businesses,

00:16:06.440 --> 00:16:07.912
or from your IoT.

00:16:07.912 --> 00:16:10.000
I have chosen to make
just four examples,

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:13.330
but you can have a
very big list of data.

00:16:13.330 --> 00:16:17.200
At this day, you can see that
we have almost 80 different data

00:16:17.200 --> 00:16:20.540
sources around the world.

00:16:20.540 --> 00:16:22.930
And after we have
acquired this data,

00:16:22.930 --> 00:16:27.160
we store this without
modification and cloud storage,

00:16:27.160 --> 00:16:29.950
because we think it's
very important to have

00:16:29.950 --> 00:16:34.180
a history of each data we will
modify after all this step.

00:16:34.180 --> 00:16:36.100
And as you probably
know, the storage

00:16:36.100 --> 00:16:37.960
is very cheap
today on the cloud,

00:16:37.960 --> 00:16:42.890
so why don't just put the
storage on cloud storage

00:16:42.890 --> 00:16:46.920
and have this if sometimes
you need to go back,

00:16:46.920 --> 00:16:49.900
or a modification,
at least to check.

00:16:49.900 --> 00:16:53.780
This represents about 50
terabytes of storage today.

00:16:53.780 --> 00:16:57.470
And after all this, we make
the most interesting point.

00:16:57.470 --> 00:17:00.280
This is the analyze
and processing part.

00:17:00.280 --> 00:17:01.680
We use the classic--

00:17:01.680 --> 00:17:07.060
as I say, "classic"
pipeline data from GCB.

00:17:07.060 --> 00:17:09.000
We use Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:17:09.000 --> 00:17:11.390
Cloud Pub/Sub is
notifying dataflow.

00:17:11.390 --> 00:17:14.349
We process dataflow
every month a lot.

00:17:14.349 --> 00:17:17.740
You can see some numbers
of the usage we have.

00:17:17.740 --> 00:17:20.069
And we send this
data to be queried.

00:17:20.069 --> 00:17:22.750
This represents about
60 billion [INAUDIBLE]..

00:17:22.750 --> 00:17:26.319
And we are very happy
to use BigQuery.

00:17:26.319 --> 00:17:29.830
We have a big team of
experts on BigQuery,

00:17:29.830 --> 00:17:33.670
and after this we can choose to
export this data and bring them

00:17:33.670 --> 00:17:37.690
to external application, web
application on Google Cloud,

00:17:37.690 --> 00:17:38.890
on-premise application--

00:17:38.890 --> 00:17:40.870
Data Studio is an
example, if you

00:17:40.870 --> 00:17:43.720
want to make some dashboard,
because we used it as a tool

00:17:43.720 --> 00:17:46.900
to make some
interesting dashboard.

00:17:46.900 --> 00:17:50.010
Where you want, because
you can open a PI

00:17:50.010 --> 00:17:53.040
and give this data to
an external application,

00:17:53.040 --> 00:17:54.060
as I said.

00:17:54.060 --> 00:17:58.265
So to present you a demo
of how we use this data.

00:17:58.265 --> 00:18:00.040
I have chosen to
present you Synoptique.

00:18:00.040 --> 00:18:02.690
Synoptique is an external
module of the datalake.

00:18:02.690 --> 00:18:08.170
And it will come into Veolia
to monitor all the equipment we

00:18:08.170 --> 00:18:11.030
have on the treatment plants--
for the waste or the water,

00:18:11.030 --> 00:18:13.110
for example.

00:18:13.110 --> 00:18:16.780
So it means you will be able
to see, for example, how

00:18:16.780 --> 00:18:22.360
is the amount of water I have
in my water pump today from now,

00:18:22.360 --> 00:18:25.670
and also a very
interesting point.

00:18:25.670 --> 00:18:28.000
This application is
made on three steps.

00:18:28.000 --> 00:18:31.480
First is acquire the
data from the datalake,

00:18:31.480 --> 00:18:35.620
and we get an SVG representation
of all the equipments you will

00:18:35.620 --> 00:18:38.720
have on the treatment plants.

00:18:38.720 --> 00:18:40.770
Second step is to prepare this.

00:18:40.770 --> 00:18:43.800
So we merge the data of
the datalake with the SVG

00:18:43.800 --> 00:18:47.220
representation to create
a final representation we

00:18:47.220 --> 00:18:48.450
will present with App Engine.

00:18:51.320 --> 00:18:56.520
After this, you can see
how this application is.

00:18:56.520 --> 00:18:58.470
This application
is on App Engine,

00:18:58.470 --> 00:19:01.980
and we use Angular
framework for the front end.

00:19:01.980 --> 00:19:06.030
So Veolia's employee
can show, as I said,

00:19:06.030 --> 00:19:08.550
in real time what are
the equipments they

00:19:08.550 --> 00:19:11.060
have on these treatment plants.

00:19:11.060 --> 00:19:13.440
They will be able to
choose which city they

00:19:13.440 --> 00:19:14.344
want to monitor.

00:19:14.344 --> 00:19:16.260
In this example, we have
some cities in France

00:19:16.260 --> 00:19:18.060
and some cities in Germany.

00:19:18.060 --> 00:19:21.304
But that can be very
simple or very complex.

00:19:21.304 --> 00:19:23.220
It depends of the size
of the treatment plants

00:19:23.220 --> 00:19:24.720
and what they are doing.

00:19:24.720 --> 00:19:27.600
So you have a lot
of information.

00:19:27.600 --> 00:19:29.820
They are loaded
from the datalake

00:19:29.820 --> 00:19:31.050
every minute or second--

00:19:31.050 --> 00:19:32.550
it depends on what you need.

00:19:32.550 --> 00:19:34.650
And you can click
on an equipment

00:19:34.650 --> 00:19:37.200
and have more detailed
information on what

00:19:37.200 --> 00:19:38.610
you have on this equipment.

00:19:38.610 --> 00:19:42.510
And as you see in my video, I
have clicked on an Information,

00:19:42.510 --> 00:19:44.920
and I have a history
of this data.

00:19:44.920 --> 00:19:47.160
So it's requesting
BigQuery query

00:19:47.160 --> 00:19:50.490
to say, can you give me the
history of this information

00:19:50.490 --> 00:19:51.840
from the last months.

00:19:51.840 --> 00:19:55.120
You can use this data
just for your information,

00:19:55.120 --> 00:19:58.080
or you can use an export
of this information.

00:19:58.080 --> 00:20:02.530
So a basic front-end
application, but a very complex

00:20:02.530 --> 00:20:04.900
back-end application.

00:20:04.900 --> 00:20:08.600
So my second example
is not on the same web,

00:20:08.600 --> 00:20:12.400
but it's very interesting
because it's an application--

00:20:12.400 --> 00:20:13.840
its name is CLEAR--

00:20:13.840 --> 00:20:17.080
which permits to monitor the
gas dispersion in the atmosphere

00:20:17.080 --> 00:20:21.250
you have provided, and
makes by treatment plants.

00:20:21.250 --> 00:20:24.670
And if you live near
a treatment plant,

00:20:24.670 --> 00:20:28.200
you can be afraid if there
is not a problem today.

00:20:28.200 --> 00:20:30.940
Maybe in the industry sometimes
you can have a problem,

00:20:30.940 --> 00:20:33.250
and this application
is here to confirm

00:20:33.250 --> 00:20:35.970
that there is not a problem.

00:20:35.970 --> 00:20:38.100
Why this application
is very interesting?

00:20:38.100 --> 00:20:41.190
Because they use
both cloud providers.

00:20:41.190 --> 00:20:44.130
And you see that I
have made the icon.

00:20:44.130 --> 00:20:46.650
Thank you, Ameet.

00:20:46.650 --> 00:20:49.597
First, we collect the
data by two ways--

00:20:49.597 --> 00:20:51.180
get the data from
the weather station,

00:20:51.180 --> 00:20:54.540
because they can have
some wind or some rain.

00:20:54.540 --> 00:20:56.970
And we get the data
from local sensors

00:20:56.970 --> 00:20:59.070
all around the treatment plants.

00:20:59.070 --> 00:21:02.340
This is on the
Veolia responsibility

00:21:02.340 --> 00:21:06.420
to say if they want to have
10 or five local sensors.

00:21:06.420 --> 00:21:08.850
But it depends what
you want to monitor.

00:21:08.850 --> 00:21:11.200
After this, we store
and we simulate.

00:21:11.200 --> 00:21:13.020
We generate the panache.

00:21:13.020 --> 00:21:18.270
The panache is the cloud
representation of this gas

00:21:18.270 --> 00:21:20.770
dispersion in the atmosphere.

00:21:20.770 --> 00:21:25.030
We send this data to Google
Cloud to create with Google Map

00:21:25.030 --> 00:21:28.590
the representation we will
display on App Engine.

00:21:28.590 --> 00:21:30.420
As I said, we use a
lot of App Engine,

00:21:30.420 --> 00:21:34.210
and you can see that
it's confirming.

00:21:34.210 --> 00:21:37.290
This is the demo of
this application.

00:21:37.290 --> 00:21:41.740
In green, you see the points are
the local sensors you can find

00:21:41.740 --> 00:21:43.960
around the treatment plants.

00:21:43.960 --> 00:21:46.360
You can choose different
types of sensor

00:21:46.360 --> 00:21:48.740
and different types of gas.

00:21:48.740 --> 00:21:51.830
And in red, you can
see in real time

00:21:51.830 --> 00:21:54.860
the representation
of this dispersion.

00:21:54.860 --> 00:21:56.600
This was the first
version, and now we

00:21:56.600 --> 00:21:58.760
are on the new version
of the application

00:21:58.760 --> 00:22:01.520
and the cloud is more beautiful.

00:22:01.520 --> 00:22:03.660
For the first version,
it was not very,

00:22:03.660 --> 00:22:07.670
very the most beautiful we can,
but it has evolved from now.

00:22:07.670 --> 00:22:11.160
You can have the
version of the maps,

00:22:11.160 --> 00:22:14.760
and you can see if your
home is at the right.

00:22:14.760 --> 00:22:16.530
Maybe you have
recognized your street.

00:22:16.530 --> 00:22:17.670
I don't know.

00:22:17.670 --> 00:22:21.430
And you have a different type
of information, as I said.

00:22:21.430 --> 00:22:23.670
It can be very
technical so that you

00:22:23.670 --> 00:22:28.800
can have also technical
information about each sensor--

00:22:28.800 --> 00:22:33.810
about where they are, about what
the data they have collected.

00:22:33.810 --> 00:22:36.540
You have a historical of
this data, also taking

00:22:36.540 --> 00:22:39.000
from BigQuery,
from the datalake.

00:22:39.000 --> 00:22:42.150
And like Synoptic,
you can use this data

00:22:42.150 --> 00:22:46.270
to make an export for use in
another application, of course.

00:22:46.270 --> 00:22:52.530
These are very, very easy-to-use
applications, and not

00:22:52.530 --> 00:22:57.070
complex to understand
how to use it.

00:22:57.070 --> 00:23:01.230
So now it's time to speak
with Ameet about BeyondCorp,

00:23:01.230 --> 00:23:04.358
and after I will be
back here on this.

00:23:04.358 --> 00:23:07.746
[APPLAUSE]

00:23:11.469 --> 00:23:12.760
AMEET JANI: Thank you, Antoine.

00:23:12.760 --> 00:23:13.985
So the question
you're asking is,

00:23:13.985 --> 00:23:15.430
he's created all
this great data,

00:23:15.430 --> 00:23:16.840
he's created all these
great front ends.

00:23:16.840 --> 00:23:19.214
How does he make sure that
the right people are accessing

00:23:19.214 --> 00:23:20.389
this kind of information?

00:23:20.389 --> 00:23:22.180
And so to do that I
thought I could quickly

00:23:22.180 --> 00:23:24.670
give you a quick summary
of what BeyondCorp is.

00:23:24.670 --> 00:23:27.550
About 10 years ago,
Google decided internally

00:23:27.550 --> 00:23:29.230
that we wanted to
get rid of VPNs.

00:23:29.230 --> 00:23:31.630
We decided there was this idea
that no network was really

00:23:31.630 --> 00:23:34.870
truly safe, and in fact
the way to really guarantee

00:23:34.870 --> 00:23:35.770
that every access--

00:23:35.770 --> 00:23:38.020
every request-- is
actually legitimate,

00:23:38.020 --> 00:23:40.300
to do an evaluation
of each request.

00:23:40.300 --> 00:23:43.420
And so from that point,
we've moved all of our tools

00:23:43.420 --> 00:23:47.620
from behind a VPN into the
world, to put it on the web.

00:23:47.620 --> 00:23:49.750
This is including things
like our code search,

00:23:49.750 --> 00:23:51.250
where we store our
search algorithms

00:23:51.250 --> 00:23:52.670
and things like that.

00:23:52.670 --> 00:23:54.800
And the idea should be that--

00:23:54.800 --> 00:23:57.250
that's false security, and
we should make a request

00:23:57.250 --> 00:24:00.269
on how everybody's ad
request looks at that time.

00:24:00.269 --> 00:24:01.810
So let me walk you
through an example

00:24:01.810 --> 00:24:03.490
of what that looks like.

00:24:03.490 --> 00:24:06.500
In our BeyondCorp idea, we
think of really three things.

00:24:06.500 --> 00:24:09.930
One is that you, the enterprise,
need to set policies.

00:24:09.930 --> 00:24:12.970
These are rich
policies that say,

00:24:12.970 --> 00:24:15.010
these are high-trust
applications.

00:24:15.010 --> 00:24:17.570
Maybe these are cafe menus,
anybody can access it.

00:24:17.570 --> 00:24:19.361
Those are the kinds of
rich rules you have.

00:24:19.361 --> 00:24:20.760
And what does that mean?

00:24:20.760 --> 00:24:23.110
What IP address
am I coming from?

00:24:23.110 --> 00:24:25.512
What does my device look like?

00:24:25.512 --> 00:24:26.470
What's the time of day?

00:24:26.470 --> 00:24:27.520
These are the
types of attributes

00:24:27.520 --> 00:24:29.890
that we want to collect when
somebody is making a request.

00:24:29.890 --> 00:24:31.240
And then going through
and cataloging,

00:24:31.240 --> 00:24:33.010
what are the applications
and data that we have,

00:24:33.010 --> 00:24:34.210
and making sure
they're all protected.

00:24:34.210 --> 00:24:36.070
And so that all those
three components

00:24:36.070 --> 00:24:37.750
constitute what we
call BeyondCorp,

00:24:37.750 --> 00:24:39.250
or the BeyondCorp vision.

00:24:39.250 --> 00:24:41.420
And so let's walk through
a typical example.

00:24:41.420 --> 00:24:43.810
There's no
revolutionary stuff here

00:24:43.810 --> 00:24:45.400
if you look at it
at first blush.

00:24:45.400 --> 00:24:48.190
An employee uses some sort
of device to make sure

00:24:48.190 --> 00:24:50.180
that the rules are
processed here that say,

00:24:50.180 --> 00:24:52.015
is this really a
legitimate request.

00:24:52.015 --> 00:24:53.980
It's blocked at an
enforcement point,

00:24:53.980 --> 00:24:56.320
in which case, in our case
we think of it as a proxy.

00:24:56.320 --> 00:24:58.090
And if everything
matches, we in fact

00:24:58.090 --> 00:25:01.754
allow that end user to access
the application or the data.

00:25:01.754 --> 00:25:03.670
That sounds simple enough,
but a lot of things

00:25:03.670 --> 00:25:05.461
can go wrong-- and do
go wrong-- for people

00:25:05.461 --> 00:25:06.920
who try to go at this point.

00:25:06.920 --> 00:25:08.380
The first one is,
how do you really

00:25:08.380 --> 00:25:10.630
attest that this is actually
the employee who's trying

00:25:10.630 --> 00:25:11.770
to access the information.

00:25:11.770 --> 00:25:12.275
Right?

00:25:12.275 --> 00:25:13.900
In terms of phished
credentials, if you

00:25:13.900 --> 00:25:16.360
think of their entire effect,
the number one attack vector

00:25:16.360 --> 00:25:17.990
used to access information.

00:25:17.990 --> 00:25:20.260
It's very easy to mimic
who an employee is.

00:25:20.260 --> 00:25:23.549
Second, even if the user is
legitimate, what is the device?

00:25:23.549 --> 00:25:25.090
Is the device in a
compromised state?

00:25:25.090 --> 00:25:26.479
Is it a corporate device?

00:25:26.479 --> 00:25:27.520
You know, is it a tablet?

00:25:27.520 --> 00:25:29.039
What is it that's
going on there?

00:25:29.039 --> 00:25:30.580
The next thing you
have to figure out

00:25:30.580 --> 00:25:33.190
is, how do I make sure
that the rules I've

00:25:33.190 --> 00:25:35.220
applied against my
applications are legitimate?

00:25:35.220 --> 00:25:36.630
Are they over-privileged?

00:25:36.630 --> 00:25:38.394
Is the network path vulnerable?

00:25:38.394 --> 00:25:40.810
And then you have to collect
all this information together

00:25:40.810 --> 00:25:44.800
and see, should an access
decision be made or not,

00:25:44.800 --> 00:25:47.680
before the access decision
is actually allowed.

00:25:47.680 --> 00:25:49.740
And in each of these, we
do this on every request

00:25:49.740 --> 00:25:51.130
to an application,
so the latency

00:25:51.130 --> 00:25:53.730
has to be near zero in order
to not frustrate your customer,

00:25:53.730 --> 00:25:53.890
right?

00:25:53.890 --> 00:25:56.181
You're replacing a VPN, but
if you're putting something

00:25:56.181 --> 00:25:57.730
in that's much
heavier, you in fact

00:25:57.730 --> 00:26:00.014
create more headaches
for your end users.

00:26:00.014 --> 00:26:01.930
And so what we've decided
to do is attack each

00:26:01.930 --> 00:26:04.130
of these verticals with
multiple solutions.

00:26:04.130 --> 00:26:06.370
The first one is, you need
to have a strong identity

00:26:06.370 --> 00:26:06.770
provider.

00:26:06.770 --> 00:26:07.935
And this is not an
identity provider talk,

00:26:07.935 --> 00:26:09.020
so I'm not going
to dive into that.

00:26:09.020 --> 00:26:11.470
But we need to attest that
this is in fact a strong user.

00:26:11.470 --> 00:26:13.220
But we want to go one
step further, right?

00:26:13.220 --> 00:26:16.840
If you talk about second
factor, most second factors

00:26:16.840 --> 00:26:18.800
are actually phishable
credentials, right,

00:26:18.800 --> 00:26:19.480
or bare tokens.

00:26:19.480 --> 00:26:21.520
And so we need to have
a phishing-resistant

00:26:21.520 --> 00:26:22.750
authentication that we use.

00:26:22.750 --> 00:26:27.104
And we at Google use the
touch key, the U2F keys.

00:26:27.104 --> 00:26:29.020
The next thing is on the
device itself, right?

00:26:29.020 --> 00:26:32.120
If it's a mobile device, we
want strong MDM on the device.

00:26:32.120 --> 00:26:34.630
If it's a laptop, we need
to ensure that the device is

00:26:34.630 --> 00:26:35.680
actually in a good state.

00:26:35.680 --> 00:26:37.100
And you can do that in
several different ways,

00:26:37.100 --> 00:26:38.330
and we can talk to those.

00:26:38.330 --> 00:26:40.280
The first one is around
device management.

00:26:40.280 --> 00:26:42.780
You need to make sure that we
have a well-configured TLS us

00:26:42.780 --> 00:26:43.863
when you make the request.

00:26:43.863 --> 00:26:45.840
That's an obvious attack vector.

00:26:45.840 --> 00:26:47.410
You have to have a
very simple rules

00:26:47.410 --> 00:26:48.701
engine when you do all of this.

00:26:48.701 --> 00:26:50.120
When you put all
of this together,

00:26:50.120 --> 00:26:51.965
it needs to be very
clear to an admin

00:26:51.965 --> 00:26:53.090
or to a [INAUDIBLE] person.

00:26:53.090 --> 00:26:54.339
Am I building the right thing?

00:26:54.339 --> 00:26:55.600
Am I doing the right thing?

00:26:55.600 --> 00:26:57.989
Are these applied correctly
against the application?

00:26:57.989 --> 00:26:59.530
And then of course
we have the proxy,

00:26:59.530 --> 00:27:01.070
where the access
controls happen,

00:27:01.070 --> 00:27:02.970
and then of course
we terminate the TLS.

00:27:02.970 --> 00:27:04.755
And assuming everything
is right there,

00:27:04.755 --> 00:27:06.880
then of course we allow
access to the applications.

00:27:06.880 --> 00:27:08.919
That could be applications,
it can be data.

00:27:08.919 --> 00:27:10.460
But think about this
new cloud world.

00:27:10.460 --> 00:27:14.064
No matter what cloud you use,
one of the biggest attack

00:27:14.064 --> 00:27:16.480
vectors that are out there--
these are public APIs, right?

00:27:16.480 --> 00:27:18.146
If you want to write
to a storage bucket

00:27:18.146 --> 00:27:19.137
that is a public API.

00:27:19.137 --> 00:27:21.220
And so you need to be able
to extend your security

00:27:21.220 --> 00:27:23.020
boundary around those APIs.

00:27:23.020 --> 00:27:25.000
And so we have solutions
that we're introducing

00:27:25.000 --> 00:27:26.737
around that space too.

00:27:26.737 --> 00:27:29.320
And so I thought maybe it makes
sense to talk to these briefly

00:27:29.320 --> 00:27:30.400
before I give it
back to Antoine.

00:27:30.400 --> 00:27:32.094
But the first one
that we did at Google

00:27:32.094 --> 00:27:34.510
was to introduce this idea of
an identity database, right?

00:27:34.510 --> 00:27:37.024
It has to have a
rich set of metadata,

00:27:37.024 --> 00:27:39.190
which includes the users,
the group memberships, job

00:27:39.190 --> 00:27:42.410
titles, offices, where is
somebody coming in from.

00:27:42.410 --> 00:27:44.050
That's all par for
course, I think.

00:27:44.050 --> 00:27:45.690
The next thing you
need to have is,

00:27:45.690 --> 00:27:47.660
you need to have
strong authentication.

00:27:47.660 --> 00:27:49.701
These are primary credentials,
but these are also

00:27:49.701 --> 00:27:50.710
secondary credentials.

00:27:50.710 --> 00:27:52.800
Think of the U2F keys
and things like that

00:27:52.800 --> 00:27:55.240
so that you can actually test
that this is the right user.

00:27:55.240 --> 00:27:56.710
And they need to have
short-lived sessions.

00:27:56.710 --> 00:27:57.970
A lot of these
vendors today have

00:27:57.970 --> 00:27:59.137
sessions that go on forever.

00:27:59.137 --> 00:28:00.636
If you're accessing
something that's

00:28:00.636 --> 00:28:02.470
a business-critical
value, clearly it makes

00:28:02.470 --> 00:28:04.510
sense that you need to have
very short-lived sessions,

00:28:04.510 --> 00:28:06.551
versus if you're accessing,
again, the cafe menu.

00:28:06.551 --> 00:28:09.140
Those sessions can live forever.

00:28:09.140 --> 00:28:11.490
The other thing that
we've introduced just

00:28:11.490 --> 00:28:14.370
in the last week is something
we call endpoint verification.

00:28:14.370 --> 00:28:18.000
Endpoint verification is this
idea that on your device itself

00:28:18.000 --> 00:28:20.820
we can take a look at the
security posture of the device.

00:28:20.820 --> 00:28:23.000
Is this the user's
right serial number?

00:28:23.000 --> 00:28:25.230
Is the OS version up to date?

00:28:25.230 --> 00:28:26.490
Is the screen lock enabled?

00:28:26.490 --> 00:28:27.531
Is there disc encryption?

00:28:27.531 --> 00:28:29.820
On and on and on, you can
capture simple metrics

00:28:29.820 --> 00:28:32.010
about the end user's
device to say, yes,

00:28:32.010 --> 00:28:32.940
this is a safe device.

00:28:32.940 --> 00:28:34.080
This is not a safe device.

00:28:34.080 --> 00:28:35.205
This is a corporate device.

00:28:35.205 --> 00:28:37.220
This is not a device--
before you allow access

00:28:37.220 --> 00:28:39.217
to applications.

00:28:39.217 --> 00:28:40.800
And then, of course,
you need a proxy.

00:28:40.800 --> 00:28:42.216
We at Google have
something called

00:28:42.216 --> 00:28:43.380
the Identity-Aware Proxy.

00:28:43.380 --> 00:28:44.838
The Identity-Aware
Proxy in essence

00:28:44.838 --> 00:28:47.250
is a large, global
scale reverse proxy

00:28:47.250 --> 00:28:49.440
that sits in front of all
of our infrastructure.

00:28:49.440 --> 00:28:51.150
And what it does
is it intercepts

00:28:51.150 --> 00:28:53.316
all of the requests that
are going to an application

00:28:53.316 --> 00:28:55.467
and makes a check.

00:28:55.467 --> 00:28:56.800
We said this was Ameet's device.

00:28:56.800 --> 00:28:58.512
It's a corporate device
for him, and he's

00:28:58.512 --> 00:28:59.970
coming in from an
IP address that I

00:28:59.970 --> 00:29:01.440
trust, let's allow access.

00:29:01.440 --> 00:29:02.940
Or most of these
things are true,

00:29:02.940 --> 00:29:04.766
but he's coming from
a public coffee shop.

00:29:04.766 --> 00:29:07.140
Maybe we should upgrade and
ask for a second factor here,

00:29:07.140 --> 00:29:07.639
right?

00:29:07.639 --> 00:29:09.760
And so those are the
kinds of decisions

00:29:09.760 --> 00:29:12.700
you could start to make
at the proxy layer.

00:29:12.700 --> 00:29:15.630
And then of course, how do
you bring this all together?

00:29:15.630 --> 00:29:19.320
You need to have what we call
an access control engine.

00:29:19.320 --> 00:29:22.560
This is where you can start to
define simple rules that say,

00:29:22.560 --> 00:29:24.240
I've classified all
of the applications

00:29:24.240 --> 00:29:27.300
into really, really secure,
OK to leak information,

00:29:27.300 --> 00:29:28.890
and not secure at all.

00:29:28.890 --> 00:29:30.570
I need to define what
those mean, right?

00:29:30.570 --> 00:29:31.710
I need to understand
that, if I'm

00:29:31.710 --> 00:29:33.793
coming in for a really,
really secure application,

00:29:33.793 --> 00:29:35.850
it needs to come in from
a trusted IP address.

00:29:35.850 --> 00:29:36.685
I can set that.

00:29:36.685 --> 00:29:38.310
I can say that, in
fact, I need to know

00:29:38.310 --> 00:29:40.435
that it's a Chromebook,
because I trust Chromebooks

00:29:40.435 --> 00:29:41.670
more than I trust others--

00:29:41.670 --> 00:29:44.820
in our case-- so I want to
grant access to that device

00:29:44.820 --> 00:29:47.460
more than I would, for example,
a Mac or a Windows device.

00:29:47.460 --> 00:29:49.043
And you can start
to see how you would

00:29:49.043 --> 00:29:50.764
be able to create these rules.

00:29:50.764 --> 00:29:52.680
You have thousands and
thousands and thousands

00:29:52.680 --> 00:29:54.260
of internal applications.

00:29:54.260 --> 00:29:55.968
All right, well at
least we at Google do,

00:29:55.968 --> 00:29:58.620
and I'm sure many of you have
the same order of magnitude.

00:29:58.620 --> 00:30:00.870
You need to be able to find
a scalable way of applying

00:30:00.870 --> 00:30:02.786
all of these rules against
those applications.

00:30:02.786 --> 00:30:05.370
If you have to go through
and do a one by one by one

00:30:05.370 --> 00:30:06.829
kind of application
of those rules,

00:30:06.829 --> 00:30:09.078
you'll never get through it,
and you'll never actually

00:30:09.078 --> 00:30:10.270
fully roll out the model.

00:30:10.270 --> 00:30:12.990
And so what we actually have
done with our own access

00:30:12.990 --> 00:30:15.300
control engine that we're
now product testing for users

00:30:15.300 --> 00:30:18.059
is create these ideas of
simple access levels, right?

00:30:18.059 --> 00:30:20.100
To classify something is
sensitive, to access it,

00:30:20.100 --> 00:30:22.183
I need to be coming in
from a specific IP address,

00:30:22.183 --> 00:30:24.452
or I need to come in from a
specific version of an OS.

00:30:24.452 --> 00:30:26.910
If something is mostly trusted,
OK, it's coming in from me.

00:30:26.910 --> 00:30:28.980
All right, that's good enough.

00:30:28.980 --> 00:30:31.920
And it's coming in from
a mostly up-to-date OS.

00:30:31.920 --> 00:30:34.470
I'll allow that access to
what we call mostly trusted.

00:30:34.470 --> 00:30:37.910
Or if it's an end user's tablet
that they brought from home

00:30:37.910 --> 00:30:40.050
and they want to access
that data, that's OK too.

00:30:40.050 --> 00:30:44.566
They can only access a
certain tier of applications.

00:30:44.566 --> 00:30:46.440
So I wanted to walk
through an actual example

00:30:46.440 --> 00:30:47.815
of what we do at
Google, and what

00:30:47.815 --> 00:30:49.240
we're exposing to end users.

00:30:49.240 --> 00:30:49.810
So let's take this user.

00:30:49.810 --> 00:30:50.460
This is Joe.

00:30:50.460 --> 00:30:51.970
He's sitting in his office.

00:30:51.970 --> 00:30:52.920
He's using his Mac.

00:30:52.920 --> 00:30:56.077
We know the serial number, we
know the status of the device.

00:30:56.077 --> 00:30:56.910
The rules check out.

00:30:56.910 --> 00:30:58.868
It's a corporate device,
he's the right person,

00:30:58.868 --> 00:31:01.185
so he can in fact
access our bug system.

00:31:01.185 --> 00:31:04.014
So identity-- or proxy, in our
case-- for this global proxy

00:31:04.014 --> 00:31:05.430
will make that
evaluation and say,

00:31:05.430 --> 00:31:08.220
yep, he should have access
to business critical systems

00:31:08.220 --> 00:31:11.770
here where he can see
those kinds of things.

00:31:11.770 --> 00:31:13.200
Now let's take a sort corollary.

00:31:13.200 --> 00:31:15.060
This will seem obvious, but
there's resolutions here

00:31:15.060 --> 00:31:16.143
that we should talk about.

00:31:16.143 --> 00:31:19.710
Again, this is Joe, but
now Joe's using his tablet.

00:31:19.710 --> 00:31:23.814
And so the rules in general
say, well, this is Joe, yes.

00:31:23.814 --> 00:31:25.980
It's Joe's tablet, I know
nothing about this device,

00:31:25.980 --> 00:31:28.350
and it's coming in from
a public IP address.

00:31:28.350 --> 00:31:30.580
I can't allow access
to that system, right?

00:31:30.580 --> 00:31:35.724
And so in this case, the proxy
will say, no, deny the access.

00:31:35.724 --> 00:31:37.140
That's an obvious
kind of workflow

00:31:37.140 --> 00:31:38.452
that we're talking about here.

00:31:38.452 --> 00:31:40.410
But let's talk about some
of the details, here.

00:31:40.410 --> 00:31:43.230
You can start to make
this a richer use case,

00:31:43.230 --> 00:31:45.090
here, where you can
say maybe this is Joe,

00:31:45.090 --> 00:31:46.722
maybe this is Joe's
tablet and it's

00:31:46.722 --> 00:31:48.180
coming in from a
public IP address,

00:31:48.180 --> 00:31:49.606
but he still needs
to do his job.

00:31:49.606 --> 00:31:51.480
He still needs to be
able to access systems--

00:31:51.480 --> 00:31:53.190
maybe not the bug tracking
system, but definitely

00:31:53.190 --> 00:31:54.450
his calendaring
system, and definitely

00:31:54.450 --> 00:31:56.730
his email system-- whatever--
definitely his HR systems,

00:31:56.730 --> 00:31:58.290
so he can take the time
off if he needs to.

00:31:58.290 --> 00:32:00.270
But you can start to
come back and set rules

00:32:00.270 --> 00:32:02.102
that say, OK, this is
Joe and Joe's tablet,

00:32:02.102 --> 00:32:03.810
and I don't know much
about Joe's tablet,

00:32:03.810 --> 00:32:06.024
but if he presents a
legitimate second factor

00:32:06.024 --> 00:32:08.190
then we can in fact allow
him access to what we call

00:32:08.190 --> 00:32:10.830
middle-tier applications like
calendaring and other kinds

00:32:10.830 --> 00:32:11.760
of systems.

00:32:11.760 --> 00:32:14.340
That's how we at Google resolve
these kinds of problems.

00:32:14.340 --> 00:32:15.660
And these are the kinds
of systems that we're now

00:32:15.660 --> 00:32:18.660
exposing with our Identity-Aware
Proxy and other set of tools

00:32:18.660 --> 00:32:19.919
for customers to use.

00:32:19.919 --> 00:32:21.960
So I'll give it back to
Antoine to talk about how

00:32:21.960 --> 00:32:23.001
Veolia uses those things.

00:32:23.001 --> 00:32:24.700
ANTOINE CASTEX: Thank you.

00:32:24.700 --> 00:32:27.720
[APPLAUSE]

00:32:27.720 --> 00:32:29.510
Thank you, Ameet.

00:32:29.510 --> 00:32:31.730
It was very funny because
when we are providing

00:32:31.730 --> 00:32:35.450
this presentation, we made
the slide on each side,

00:32:35.450 --> 00:32:37.730
and when are
discussing Ameet said,

00:32:37.730 --> 00:32:40.520
hey, you have almost
the same slides as me.

00:32:40.520 --> 00:32:43.370
So we say, we have the same
problem and the same security

00:32:43.370 --> 00:32:45.040
concerns.

00:32:45.040 --> 00:32:47.280
These are on four points.

00:32:47.280 --> 00:32:50.640
First is the identity
of your user.

00:32:50.640 --> 00:32:53.240
Are they the person
they pretend to be?

00:32:53.240 --> 00:32:57.200
You need to be sure that the
device is not compromised,

00:32:57.200 --> 00:33:00.260
and you need to be sure
the data transport is good.

00:33:00.260 --> 00:33:01.905
And of course, the
application, if this

00:33:01.905 --> 00:33:04.160
is well protected or not.

00:33:04.160 --> 00:33:06.580
So how BeyondCorp--
and thank you again,

00:33:06.580 --> 00:33:09.110
Ameet, and all the
BeyondCorp teams for the help

00:33:09.110 --> 00:33:11.420
you provide to Veolia.

00:33:11.420 --> 00:33:13.420
How BeyondCorp
helps us every day?

00:33:13.420 --> 00:33:16.850
I have chosen to resume a very
quick list of the services

00:33:16.850 --> 00:33:22.010
we use every day to secure and
monitor our GCP infrastructure.

00:33:22.010 --> 00:33:26.030
First, near the Veolia logo
is the cloud security command

00:33:26.030 --> 00:33:26.840
center.

00:33:26.840 --> 00:33:28.820
This is a very
new service, and I

00:33:28.820 --> 00:33:30.680
recommend you to
test it because it

00:33:30.680 --> 00:33:34.460
will be very helpful to
have on every central point

00:33:34.460 --> 00:33:37.190
all the external
services of security

00:33:37.190 --> 00:33:39.860
you can choose to have,
and inventory of all

00:33:39.860 --> 00:33:42.409
the objects you have on GCP.

00:33:42.409 --> 00:33:43.950
On the first line
you see BeyondCorp,

00:33:43.950 --> 00:33:46.380
because BeyondCorp is
also a team which is

00:33:46.380 --> 00:33:48.990
making some great white papers.

00:33:48.990 --> 00:33:50.230
You can read them.

00:33:50.230 --> 00:33:52.270
They are very interesting.

00:33:52.270 --> 00:33:55.940
You have also the cloud resource
manager with the folder,

00:33:55.940 --> 00:33:57.850
the custom rules--
the AAM rules.

00:33:57.850 --> 00:34:00.630
And you have a big
and a lot of work

00:34:00.630 --> 00:34:05.250
to do if you want to be
the most effective company.

00:34:05.250 --> 00:34:07.650
On the second line you
have the GCP billing,

00:34:07.650 --> 00:34:10.320
because by the
billing, we think we

00:34:10.320 --> 00:34:14.010
can monitor if the usages of
the platform are good or not.

00:34:14.010 --> 00:34:15.670
We can detect some fraud.

00:34:15.670 --> 00:34:18.420
Maybe if on Monday
morning, I see

00:34:18.420 --> 00:34:21.600
people which are using
a lot of BigQuery,

00:34:21.600 --> 00:34:24.630
maybe this is normal because
we have special workers,

00:34:24.630 --> 00:34:27.690
or maybe this is not normal if
the people don't know the best

00:34:27.690 --> 00:34:29.610
way to request BigQuery.

00:34:29.610 --> 00:34:33.760
So the billing is the
key to monitor this.

00:34:33.760 --> 00:34:35.780
Second is the VPC.

00:34:35.780 --> 00:34:37.469
VPC service control
is the new service

00:34:37.469 --> 00:34:41.460
which permits you to
secure and separate

00:34:41.460 --> 00:34:43.350
some different
services of a project,

00:34:43.350 --> 00:34:47.880
and lets communicate just the
two BigQuery services of two

00:34:47.880 --> 00:34:49.080
different projects.

00:34:49.080 --> 00:34:51.929
You have a lot of different
ways to use these services,

00:34:51.929 --> 00:34:54.719
but it's very interesting
and very important.

00:34:54.719 --> 00:34:57.090
On the third line,
you have cloud AAP.

00:34:57.090 --> 00:35:01.140
And if you have seen on my two
applications on the diagram,

00:35:01.140 --> 00:35:07.480
we use AAP on every app
engine services we create.

00:35:07.480 --> 00:35:09.940
Security scanner, which
is related to app engine.

00:35:09.940 --> 00:35:11.800
It permits you to
scan your application

00:35:11.800 --> 00:35:15.250
and check if you have some
vulnerability on your code.

00:35:15.250 --> 00:35:17.090
And after this,
on the third line

00:35:17.090 --> 00:35:20.520
you have more the user
and employees part,

00:35:20.520 --> 00:35:24.100
with the security key
enforcement with the two steps.

00:35:24.100 --> 00:35:27.430
The Chromebook migration,
because migrate to a Chromebook

00:35:27.430 --> 00:35:30.640
is a little bit more
secure than a laptop.

00:35:30.640 --> 00:35:34.250
I will not mention a
certain operating system.

00:35:34.250 --> 00:35:36.730
The key management services
for the application

00:35:36.730 --> 00:35:39.770
to store and secure your key.

00:35:39.770 --> 00:35:43.180
And on the last two
points, they are for me

00:35:43.180 --> 00:35:46.090
the two most interesting,
because in my job

00:35:46.090 --> 00:35:47.400
I use them every day.

00:35:47.400 --> 00:35:49.780
The first is Security Monkey.

00:35:49.780 --> 00:35:52.260
Security Monkey is an
open-source product provided

00:35:52.260 --> 00:35:56.240
by Netflix, and it permits you
to monitor your infrastructure

00:35:56.240 --> 00:36:01.036
from AWS and GCP on
just the same dashboard.

00:36:01.036 --> 00:36:02.410
So it's very
helpful if you don't

00:36:02.410 --> 00:36:05.120
want to have two
different solutions.

00:36:05.120 --> 00:36:07.210
And the last one is ForCity.

00:36:07.210 --> 00:36:09.650
And yesterday I was
with the ForCity team,

00:36:09.650 --> 00:36:12.130
and I recommend you to
test this because it's

00:36:12.130 --> 00:36:16.019
a new security open-source
project sponsored by Google.

00:36:16.019 --> 00:36:17.560
It's permits you to
have an inventory

00:36:17.560 --> 00:36:19.372
of all the objects you have.

00:36:19.372 --> 00:36:22.040
It permits you to
detect some violation

00:36:22.040 --> 00:36:25.400
you have on your infrastructure,
and to enforce some policy.

00:36:25.400 --> 00:36:29.525
Maybe, for example,
if I think that I

00:36:29.525 --> 00:36:32.260
have port 25 open
on any instance

00:36:32.260 --> 00:36:34.600
I have on compute
engine is a problem,

00:36:34.600 --> 00:36:39.440
I say, when you detect
this port is open, just

00:36:39.440 --> 00:36:44.900
quickly direct this false, so
you don't care about the rest.

00:36:44.900 --> 00:36:48.900
So now you know the
different services we use.

00:36:48.900 --> 00:36:53.670
We have a new project
and we are working today

00:36:53.670 --> 00:36:56.460
with BeyondCorp teams on this.

00:36:56.460 --> 00:37:00.500
They are coming in Paris
to understand what we need,

00:37:00.500 --> 00:37:05.220
and we are improving
today and still tomorrow

00:37:05.220 --> 00:37:06.900
on these parts,
because now we want

00:37:06.900 --> 00:37:09.480
to open our application
to our customers.

00:37:09.480 --> 00:37:12.960
But you know, it's very
complicated and not

00:37:12.960 --> 00:37:16.100
easy to create an identity.

00:37:16.100 --> 00:37:17.730
On G Suite, for
example, if you want

00:37:17.730 --> 00:37:21.420
to let authenticate
your customers,

00:37:21.420 --> 00:37:25.780
sometimes you don't want to
have to monitor this user,

00:37:25.780 --> 00:37:27.480
and of course you
don't want to pay

00:37:27.480 --> 00:37:29.430
a license for a
user which will be

00:37:29.430 --> 00:37:30.850
connecting on your application.

00:37:30.850 --> 00:37:33.600
So what's your ID?

00:37:33.600 --> 00:37:36.810
Your ID is very simple, and I
have made a very interesting

00:37:36.810 --> 00:37:37.840
example.

00:37:37.840 --> 00:37:41.190
Imagine a case where Veolia
is in charge of the water

00:37:41.190 --> 00:37:43.860
treatment of Los Angeles.

00:37:43.860 --> 00:37:46.020
I am the mayor of
the city, and you

00:37:46.020 --> 00:37:49.300
agree that I have a
very good promotion.

00:37:49.300 --> 00:37:53.320
So Veolia gives me the URL
of the application Clear

00:37:53.320 --> 00:37:55.120
that I have presented
to you before.

00:37:55.120 --> 00:37:56.980
So I need to connect
on this application

00:37:56.980 --> 00:38:00.720
to check if everything
is OK in Los Angeles.

00:38:00.720 --> 00:38:02.640
I'm connecting to the URL.

00:38:02.640 --> 00:38:08.230
AAP says, stop, I'm here
to check who you are.

00:38:08.230 --> 00:38:11.770
They bring me to a portal,
a very simple page developed

00:38:11.770 --> 00:38:15.470
by Veolia, where I just need to
say if I'm an internal people,

00:38:15.470 --> 00:38:18.400
or if I'm customers.

00:38:18.400 --> 00:38:21.280
On this example, I will
say I'm a customer.

00:38:21.280 --> 00:38:23.980
And I'm redirecting
to an IdP provider.

00:38:23.980 --> 00:38:25.690
On this example, I
have chosen one login,

00:38:25.690 --> 00:38:30.196
but you can use a different
type and list of IdP provider.

00:38:30.196 --> 00:38:36.930
This IdP provider checked if
I'm existing on this database,

00:38:36.930 --> 00:38:38.020
and if yes--

00:38:38.020 --> 00:38:41.020
and that's the most interesting
part of this example--

00:38:41.020 --> 00:38:44.650
I'm connecting to this new
product of Google Cloud.

00:38:44.650 --> 00:38:49.020
This name is Cloud Identity
for Customers and Partners.

00:38:49.020 --> 00:38:50.910
maybe it will not
the final name,

00:38:50.910 --> 00:38:55.450
but I am not here to
make an announcement.

00:38:55.450 --> 00:39:01.520
But don't forget to follow this
very interesting, new product.

00:39:01.520 --> 00:39:03.060
And this product
is here for what?

00:39:03.060 --> 00:39:09.160
It's here to say, my IdP
say, OK you are present.

00:39:09.160 --> 00:39:12.040
And now I am connecting
to the cloud identity

00:39:12.040 --> 00:39:14.830
domain of the
customers of Veolia

00:39:14.830 --> 00:39:19.400
to create an identity which
will be connecting the IdP

00:39:19.400 --> 00:39:22.030
provider to this domain.

00:39:22.030 --> 00:39:24.070
And now, OK, you
have the permission

00:39:24.070 --> 00:39:25.720
to go to this application.

00:39:25.720 --> 00:39:30.400
AAP say, OK, I give
you this application.

00:39:30.400 --> 00:39:34.380
If I was click on A, I'm
internal people of Veolia,

00:39:34.380 --> 00:39:37.640
I think you probably
know what will happen.

00:39:37.640 --> 00:39:40.200
I will redirect to my
G Suite Veolia domain,

00:39:40.200 --> 00:39:45.280
and I will instantly
connect to this application.

00:39:45.280 --> 00:39:48.650
Don't forget the Cloud Identity
Customers and Partners.

00:39:48.650 --> 00:39:52.230
And I will again say
thank you to the team who

00:39:52.230 --> 00:39:53.400
is in charge of this.

00:39:53.400 --> 00:39:55.950
And I know some people of
this team are here today.

00:39:55.950 --> 00:39:58.470
And thank you, Ameet, for this.

00:39:58.470 --> 00:40:02.790
So my last slide is
more a funny slide.

00:40:02.790 --> 00:40:05.070
I recommend you to connect
with all the device you

00:40:05.070 --> 00:40:09.090
have on this URL,
ResourcingMachine.Veolia.com,

00:40:09.090 --> 00:40:11.370
now or tomorrow.

00:40:11.370 --> 00:40:13.020
You can be a
resourcer too, and you

00:40:13.020 --> 00:40:17.070
can see what type of different
application we can develop.

00:40:17.070 --> 00:40:20.190
And this is a game to permit
you to be a resourcer.

00:40:20.190 --> 00:40:22.690
It's on App Engine,
also, of course.

00:40:22.690 --> 00:40:24.180
So what's the future now?

00:40:24.180 --> 00:40:26.400
The future, as I said,
is open new applications

00:40:26.400 --> 00:40:29.430
to the customer and develop
more and more secure

00:40:29.430 --> 00:40:31.690
applications on Google Cloud.

00:40:31.690 --> 00:40:32.190
Thank you.

00:40:32.190 --> 00:40:32.790
Merci.

00:40:32.790 --> 00:40:33.690
[APPLAUSE]

00:40:33.690 --> 00:40:36.740
[MUSIC PLAYING]

